#{[17592186383430 #edb/ref 1080 "4\\sqrt{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383430 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383430 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f1836749-28a7-5fbd-bd3f-d78ad98340b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383431 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186383432 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383431 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139554" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383431 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383431 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/63/63d5de8c08559ecc88b4fd7e2184204a1e6ea2f8a9e8ca6af7959e2354876e3f.png)\n\nA $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ The corresponding ellipse and its principal axes are depicted in the diagram above. Given that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A,$ which of the following is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $9$ of ${A}^{T}A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383431 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "caf843b3-f2a1-5bdc-8d57-74ed37aa0fe2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383431 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $A$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix. Let \n\n$$\n\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\,…,{v}_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nbe an *orthonormal* basis of ${R}^{n}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}A$ with corresponding eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}\\ge …\\ge {\\lambda }_{n}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{1},$\n\n- ${\\sigma }_{2}=\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}$ is the length of $A{v}_{2},$\n\n- $⋯$\n\nIn our example, we see that\n\n- $||A{v}_{1}||=5={\\sigma }_{1},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{1}={5}^{2}=25,$ and\n\n- $||A{v}_{2}||=3={\\sigma }_{2},$ which means that ${\\lambda }_{2}={3}^{2}=9.$\n\nTherefore, an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=9$ of ${A}^{T}A$ is ${v}_{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383432 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186383433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186383432 #edb/r